Pins in-command cd tracking: the hook resolves targets against the payload cwd, but a cd segment earlier in the same command moves the base for later segments. cwd in the payload is the sandbox root, but cd sub && echo x > target.txt must resolve target.txt against sub/ (an existing tracked file, sub/target.txt) and still deny. Without cd tracking, the guard would instead resolve the bare target.txt against the payload cwd (the sandbox root) and deny on the wrong file -- the top-level target.txt, which also exists there. The expect_stdout_grep on sub/target.txt (not target.txt) is what proves the cd was actually tracked rather than the top-level file coincidentally matching.
